{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":270572,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"e9ae11b6-82e0-4715-a00b-c185a235004a","total_cost_usd":1.1965145000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64443,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":19923,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64443,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":19923,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64443,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64443},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":45406,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.045516,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":19923,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":64443,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1509985,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"As flown, the cheapest descent is a single plunge in all nine band-and-load cases, the shipped skim cooling array is wrong and band-inverted (measured suborbital third skim 0.684 against the claimed 0.972), and the return leg gets easier as the player climbs — suborbital costs 15.6% plate at full hold against low's 13.1% — while the world grid shows no configuration anywhere satisfying all eight design targets (best score 7 of 8).\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"no_interior_ablation_optimum_as_flown\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"The two ablation knobs are tuned so the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering; params assert optimal_skims of 0 (suborbital), 1 (low), 2 (high) with cost_curve minima at those counts.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cheapest_pass_count is 1 in every one of the nine band x load descents. Ablation is monotonically increasing in pass count with no interior minimum anywhere: suborbital empty 5.2 / 6.0 / 6.3 / 6.5 / 6.7 / 6.9 / 7.1 percent for 1-7 passes; suborbital full hold 15.6 / 18.0 / 18.5 / 18.9; low full hold 13.1 / 17.2 / 18.1 / 18.5; high full hold 13.6 / 18.7 / 19.5 / 19.7. The single largest step is always the first extra pass (high full hold pays 5.1 plate-percent to go from one pass to two). The flown pass sweep also raises braking_altitude_m monotonically with pass count (suborbital empty 47711 m at one pass to 66506 m at seven), i.e. it models extra passes as progressively shallower feathering rather than as k skims followed by one deeper committed entry, so the descent structure the GDD describes is not what this curve prices. The shipped cost_curve minima (high [30.49, 29.72, 29.57, 30.23], minimum at index 2) are contradicted in both shape and level by the flown figures.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_cooling_wrong_and_band_inverted\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier is a single array [1, 0.984, 0.974, 0.972] applied to every band, derived in the balance notes from a 'roughly a fifth of claimed' estimate, and it puts the most skims on the high band.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The sweep reports per-band arrays and they run the other way. Suborbital [1, 0.951, 0.897, 0.684], low [1, 0.977, 0.955, 0.934], high [1, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954]. The shipped array matches the high band only; at suborbital the third skim cools 31.6% against the 2.8% claimed, an error of a factor of eleven, and the first skim cools 4.9% against 1.6%. Substituting the measured arrays into the crew's own cost model (heat_cost_coefficient 0.000018, exponent 3, skim_peak 29, geometric toll 0.16 x 1.35^i) the marginal saving beats the marginal price at every skim tested in every band, so the optimum moves to the maximum count the sweep flew (3 skims / 4 passes) rather than to the shipped 0/1/2 — suborbital 10.45 / 9.67 / 8.97 / 5.67, low 18.16 / 17.60 / 17.22 / 17.04, high 30.49 / 29.72 / 29.07 / 28.70. The band ordering of optimal_skims is therefore backwards: suborbital skims cheapest and high skims dearest.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"difficulty_falls_with_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Reentry speed and therefore heat scale with the altitude you return from, so the value curve and the risk curve rise together (4.5 risk 2: the design's central bet). ablation.heat_index asserts a 43% spread, 83 / 100 / 119.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The return leg is hardest from the lowest band. Cheapest-descent plate cost at full hold: suborbital 15.6%, low 13.1%, high 13.6%. Empty: suborbital 5.2%, low 4.4%, high 4.5%. Peak heat at full hold: suborbital 95, low 90, high 91. So the measured heat index normalised to low = 100 is roughly 106 / 100 / 101, not 83 / 100 / 119 — the claimed 43% rise across bands is measured as a 5% fall from suborbital to high. The shipped slice's own comparison (suborbital vs low) is inverted: the cheap band is the expensive one to come home from, most likely because the suborbital entry brakes higher and longer (43374 m at full hold against 39759 m for low) and integrates more heat at a lower rate. The grid says this is fixable — difficulty_rises_with_band is satisfied by 31.8% of the 2916 configs and by all twelve top-scoring ones — so it is the world's geometry, not an impossible rule.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skimming_and_unstaged_survival_are_mutually_exclusive\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"Both halves of 2.3.1 hold at once: shallow unstaged passes are survivable with a fuelled stage still attached, and aerobraking meaningfully cools the entry so the multi-pass descent is worth flying.\",\n      \"measured\": \"No configuration in the grid buys both. Of the twelve top configs, every one that satisfies skimming_cools_the_entry fails unstaged_pass_survivable (staged beta 44.4 gives multipliers [1, 0.363, 0.363, 0.363] and unstaged peak heat 478; beta 133.3 at rho 0.5 gives [1, 0.754, 0.211, 0.211] and 371; beta 133.3 at rho 1.2 gives [1, 0.345, ...] and 523), and every one that satisfies unstaged survival fails skimming (beta 400 gives [1, 0.987, 0.974, 0.963] and unstaged 47; beta 1333 gives [1, 0.981, ...] and 59). Satisfaction rates across the whole 2916-config space: unstaged_pass_survivable 0.154, the rarest target measured, against skimming_cools_the_entry 0.441. best_score is 7 of 8 — nothing in the space satisfies all eight. The crew's config sits firmly on the unstaged-survivable side (staged beta 171.4, unstaged 734.7, unstaged shallow-pass peak heat 62 suborbital and 71 low, both well under the 100 cap) and pays for it with the weak skim cooling in the finding above. The controlling quantity is ballistic coefficient: lowering it buys skim authority and burns the unstaged hull.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"feathering_exceeds_the_heat_cap\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"reentry.heat_capacity is 100 and cargo damage begins at 100% heat, one slot every ~3 seconds, fragile at double.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Multi-pass descents at load cross the cap and the sweep still reports them as landed with the plate surviving, because the accounting is plate-only. Suborbital full hold reaches peak heat 100 at three passes and 101 at six and beyond; low full hold 100 at six passes and 101 from eleven; high full hold 101 at nine passes, 102 at ten and 102 at fourteen. The plate cost differences that separate these from a single plunge are 6-9 plate-percent, but the real price at these counts is cargo damage the ablation curve never charges. This is genuine anti-feathering pressure the params did not count, and it means the feathering side of the intended U-curve is already enforced by heat rather than by the toll.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"bands_not_gated_by_fuel\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Altitude bands gate value and reaching a band takes tank and thruster upgrades; the balance notes state they sized the base tank for reachability and could not verify whether fuel_tank tier 1 stays a wanted purchase rather than a prerequisite.\",\n      \"measured\": \"It is neither — it is unneeded for reach. At base config all three bands are reached with comfortable margin: suborbital 316.19 kg remaining (16.6%), low 255.75 kg (13.5%), high 223.80 kg (11.8%) — including the high band that 4.1 defers as out of the slice. Back-solving the burns at the params' own 2200 m/s exhaust velocity, the three ascents cost roughly 1834, 1947 and 2008 m/s against 2497 m/s available, so the entire three-band ladder is separated by about 174 m/s, or 7% of the base delta-v budget. The 2800 credits of tier-1 upgrades therefore buy margin, not access, and no purchase in the shipped tree opens a band. Grid context: fuel_margin_sane is satisfied by only 16.2% of configs and bands_reachable by 33.8%, so the crew's world is in a rare good region and this should be corrected with a small move, not a large one.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"parachute_speed_verified_but_self_referential\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.6 sits just under the 5.0 m/s soft-landing line at twice dry mass, and terminal speed scales as sqrt(m).\",\n      \"measured\": \"Holds, with one caveat. Touchdown is 4.60 m/s at full hold (901.6 kg cargo), 3.99 at half hold and 3.25 empty in all nine descents, and every one grades soft; 3.25 x sqrt(2) = 4.60 confirms the sqrt(m) law exactly. Cargo mass per slot is 150.27 kg and a full base hold is 901.6 kg against 900 kg dry, a mass ratio of 2.002, so the 2.3.5 doubling claim is measured rather than asserted. The caveat: landing.parachute_area_m2 is not in the params, so the simulator solved for the 630.1 m2 canopy implied by the 4.6 m/s claim itself. The touchdown figures are anchored to the claim, not independent of it, and the params should state the area so the next sweep can falsify it.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"some_pass_counts_are_not_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"Pass count is a continuous player choice across the whole range.\",\n      \"measured\": \"It is not, at high counts. pass_counts_reachable has gaps: low empty runs 1-11 then 13, 14, 16; low half hold 1-10 then 12, 13, 15, 17; high full hold 1-12 then 14, 15, 18, 21, 25. The gaps begin where consecutive braking altitudes converge to within a few hundred metres (low empty is at 60361 m for eleven passes and 61446 m for sixteen), i.e. the shallow end saturates and further passes stop being physically distinguishable. Minor for the shipping slice because the optimum is nowhere near these counts, but it means any UI that offers a pass-count target must clamp rather than assume every integer is flyable.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.984, 0.974, 0.972],\n      \"proposed\": {\n        \"suborbital\": [1, 0.951, 0.897, 0.684],\n        \"low\": [1, 0.977, 0.955, 0.934],\n        \"high\": [1, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954]\n      },\n      \"reason\": \"Replaces an estimate with the measured per-band arrays the balance notes explicitly asked for, and fixes an eleven-fold understatement of suborbital skim cooling; it costs the params their single-array simplicity and forces optimal_skims to be re-derived per band.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.35,\n      \"proposed\": 1.9,\n      \"reason\": \"With the measured multipliers priced through the crew's own cost model, every skim up to the third is worth taking and the optimum runs away to the shallow end; at 1.9 the third skim costs 1.537 plate-percent against a 1.201 saving at the high band and 1.012 at low, which places an interior minimum at 3 passes for low and high and 4 for suborbital, inside the 2-4 window — the cost is that a fifth pass becomes very expensive, which is the intended anti-feathering pressure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_skims\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 0, \"low\": 1, \"high\": 2},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 3, \"low\": 2, \"high\": 2},\n      \"reason\": \"This is the minimum of the repriced curve under the measured multipliers, and it corrects the band inversion — suborbital skims cheapest, not last; it costs the suborbital band its 'lazy plunge breaks even' simplicity, so the 2.3.6 break-even arithmetic needs re-running against the new suborbital cost.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.heat_index\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 83, \"low\": 100, \"high\": 119},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 106, \"low\": 100, \"high\": 101},\n      \"reason\": \"These are the measured single-pass full-hold peak heats (95 / 90 / 91) normalised to low = 100, so the params stop asserting a 43% band spread the simulator measures as a 5% fall; the cost is that it bakes the inversion into the config, which is why the baseline change below matters more.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.bands[2].altitude_min_m\",\n      \"current\": 250000,\n      \"proposed\": 400000,\n      \"reason\": \"The Researcher's own first-listed lever for a steeper return-leg curve is pushing the high band out, since it is the (R+h) term doing the work; it restores a rising risk curve for the post-semester band at the cost of a 91-minute period growing further, which makes the already-mandatory time warp mandatory sooner. It does not fix the shipped suborbital-vs-low inversion, which has no lever in the current params and belongs with the human under 4.5 risk 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 1900,\n      \"proposed\": 1600,\n      \"reason\": \"Restores a fuel-margin gradient across the bands so the tank upgrades are sold against something measurable; the cost is that at roughly 2246 m/s of ideal delta-v it very likely still opens the high band, because the three ascents are only ~174 m/s apart, so this narrows the margin rather than gating a band and must be re-flown before it is believed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": null,\n      \"proposed\": 630.1,\n      \"reason\": \"The simulator had to infer the canopy from the 4.6 m/s claim, which makes every measured touchdown speed self-confirming; stating it costs one config field and lets the next sweep test the landing rule instead of restating it.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The two halves of the verification sweep disagree about the same mechanic and I trust the skims table over the descents table. The descents pass sweep raises braking_altitude_m monotonically with pass count, so its 'more passes' means feathering at ever-shallower depth, not k skims followed by a deeper committed entry; the skims table varies the two depths separately and shows entry peak heat falling with skim count. My proposed toll change assumes the shipped ablation accounting applies skim cooling to the committed entry. If it does not, the toll change will not produce a U and the honest move is 4.5's stated fallback — a flat per-pass toll with a hard pass cap.\",\n    \"The polarity of entry_depth_m in the skims table is ambiguous. Larger values give lower entry peak heat (suborbital 100 at depth 0 against 74 at 36000), which is the opposite of what 'deeper committed entry' should do. I read all skim multipliers at entry_depth_m 0 for consistency, but if the label means the reverse of what I assumed, the multiplier arrays are measured at the shallowest entry rather than a representative one, and the cooling I priced is an upper bound.\",\n    \"The suborbital three-skim figure of 0.684 is a regime change, not smooth cooling. That case reports commit_dv_ms 0 and lands at entry peak heat 68.6 at all three entry depths — the skims alone completed the deorbit and there was no committed entry left to price. Do not extrapolate that number to four skims or to a loaded ship, and do not read it as evidence that the cooling law is steep at suborbital generally.\",\n    \"The recomputed optima and the cycle_toll_growth of 1.9 are my arithmetic through the params' own cost model, not flown results. The simulator never flew a repriced config. Every figure in that chain (savings 1.430 / 1.384 / 1.201 at high, prices 0.743 / 1.017 / 1.537) is reproducible from the params, but it is a model prediction and the next sweep should measure it rather than inherit it.\",\n    \"The fuel_capacity_kg 1600 proposal rests on rocket-equation arithmetic I did from the reported fuel remaining, not on a flown ascent at that tank size. Gravity losses change with wet mass and my back-solved per-band delta-v figures fold them in only implicitly, so the band-separation number (~174 m/s) is approximate. Treat the direction as measured and the magnitude as an estimate.\",\n    \"Nothing here speaks to feel, and several params were never exercised. The simulator is a point mass with a perfect pilot: reentry.off_retrograde_penalty, the plasma-to-chute window, latch and swing jerk, tether count and every EVA number in the params are untested by this sweep, so the 2.3.4 shear analysis in the balance notes remains unverified arithmetic. The heat cap crossings I report as cargo damage are the GDD's rule applied to the sweep's peak heat figures, not damage the simulator modelled.\",\n    \"The exploration grid's ceiling is the most important single number in it: best_score is 7 of 8 across 2916 configurations, so no world in the searched space satisfies every design target at once. That is a statement about the design's targets rather than about the crew's numbers, and it means whichever target the human chooses to drop is a pillar-level decision under 3.3, not a tuning one.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":184767,"ttft_stream_ms":3369,"time_to_request_ms":196,"type":"result","duration_ms":267729,"uuid":"7121f738-3e54-41dc-bf39-e8379e502d55"}
